From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 22:46:02 -0400
Subject: thin the toolbar and centre the URL

The reference has a thin toolbar strip with the URL centred in it. Chromium's is
46 DIP tall with the location bar filling the whole row.

Height first: the strip is as tall as its tallest child plus the interior
margin. The location bar is not that child -- the back button is -- so lowering
the location bar alone moved nothing. With the button height, the location bar
height and the margin all lowered, the strip is 43 DIP. It does not go below
that by these constants alone, because the back button's own preferred size
floors it.

Centring needed the location bar to stop wanting the whole row: with no leftover
width, spacers either side have nothing to take. SetPreferredSize() does not do
that -- FlexLayout asks for the preferred size that fits given bounds, which
goes to LocationBarView's own calculation and ignores the stored value. A flex
rule that caps the width does work, and below the cap it defers to what the bar
asks for, so a narrow window still shrinks it.

The measured result: the pill's centre is at 890 DIP against a content centre of
876, in a 1400 DIP window.

docs/UI-SPEC.md previously recorded this as deferred, on the grounds that
InitLayout() is private and non-virtual so no subclass could reach it. That was
the wrong conclusion: the change turned out to be a property on an existing
child plus two empty views, not a rewrite of the layout.

Why: the fat toolbar and the full-width omnibox were two of the four things that
made the window read as Chromium.
Removable when: the toolbar can be themed without a patch.
